const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Do Telegram Stars expire?",
    a: "No. Stars sitting in your balance do not expire and do not lose value over time. They stay there until you spend them, so there is no pressure to use them by a deadline."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I send Stars directly to a friend?",
    a: "Not as a raw balance transfer. What you can do is buy a Telegram Gift with Stars and send that, or send Stars to a channel or creator as a paid reaction or tip. To give someone a balance to spend, buy Stars addressed to their username."
  },
  {
    q: "Can I use Stars to pay for Telegram Premium?",
    a: "No. The two balances are separate and Telegram does not convert between them. Premium is bought as a subscription or received as a gift."
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need Premium to spend Stars?",
    a: "No. Stars work on any account, with or without a subscription. They are independent products."
  },
  {
    q: "What is the smallest amount of Stars I can buy?",
    a: "50 Stars, which is 12,000 UZS at the flat rate of 240 UZS per star. Any amount up to 100,000 can be entered in the bot."
  }
];
---

<BlogPostEn
  slug="what-you-can-do-with-telegram-stars"
  title="What You Can Actually Do With Telegram Stars — 8 Real Uses"
  heading="What you can actually do with Telegram Stars"
  crumb="What Stars are for"
  tag="Stars"
  description="Telegram Stars are an internal currency, but what do you spend them on? Eight concrete uses — paid posts, bots, tips, gifts, ads — and the three things Stars cannot do that people assume they can."
  keywords="what can you do with telegram stars, telegram stars uses, how to spend telegram stars, telegram stars paid posts, telegram stars gifts"
  publishDate="2026-08-07"
  uzHref="https://starsjoy.uz/blog/telegram-stars-bilan-nima-qilish-mumkin"
  ruHref="https://starsjoy.uz/ru/blog/telegram-stars-bilan-nima-qilish-mumkin"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <section>
    <p class="post-lead"><strong>Short answer:</strong> Stars are Telegram's internal currency, and everything they buy sits <em>inside</em> Telegram — paid posts, features in bots and Mini Apps, tips and paid reactions, Telegram Gifts, and advertising. They do not buy Premium, they do not transfer as a raw balance, and they do not expire.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>1. Unlocking paid posts</h2>
    <p>Channel owners can put a post behind a paywall priced in Stars. Subscribers pay once and the content unlocks for them permanently. This is the most common reason an ordinary user first needs Stars — you follow a channel, hit a locked post, and discover you have no balance.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>2. Paying inside bots and Mini Apps</h2>
    <p>Bots and Mini Apps charge in Stars for anything digital: an AI assistant's message quota, an in-game item, a subscription tier, an export in a tool. Telegram requires developers to use Stars for digital goods, which is why almost every paid bot now asks for them rather than a card.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>3. Paid reactions</h2>
    <p>On channel posts you can leave a paid reaction, which sends Stars to the channel and shows your reaction more prominently. It is the closest thing Telegram has to a tip button, built into the post itself.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>4. Tipping and supporting creators</h2>
    <p>Beyond reactions, many creators accept Stars directly as support. For the creator this is real income — Stars they earn can be withdrawn through Fragment — which is why supporting someone in Stars is meaningfully different from a like.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>5. Buying and sending Telegram Gifts</h2>
    <p>Gifts are bought with Stars and sent to another user, where they appear on the recipient's profile. Limited-edition gifts have a fixed supply and can be upgraded into collectible NFT gifts, which some people trade. The recipient can keep a gift on display or convert it back into Stars.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6. Telegram Ads</h2>
    <p>Channel owners can fund Telegram Ads campaigns with Stars. For anyone promoting a channel from Uzbekistan this matters practically: it is a route into the ad system that does not require an international card.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>7. Subscriptions inside channels</h2>
    <p>Beyond one-off paid posts, channels can run recurring Stars-based subscriptions for members-only content. The charge repeats from your Stars balance rather than from a card.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>8. Boosting your own reach as a creator</h2>
    <p>If you run a channel, holding Stars lets you participate in the same economy your subscribers do — buying gifts to run giveaways, funding ads, and paying for the bots and tools you use to produce content. Bulk purchases make sense here in a way they rarely do for individuals.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Three things Stars do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>They do not buy Telegram Premium.</strong> Separate balances, no conversion. See <a href="/en/blog/telegram-stars-or-premium">Stars or Premium</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>They do not transfer as a raw balance.</strong> You cannot send 500 Stars to a friend's balance directly — buy a gift, or buy Stars addressed to their username.</li>
      <li><strong>They do not work outside Telegram.</strong> Stars are not a general-purpose currency and have no value on any other platform.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How much to buy</h2>
    <p>The rate is flat at 240 UZS per star — there is no volume discount, so buying a large pack has no price advantage over several small ones. Buy what you need for what you are actually doing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>50–100 Stars</strong> (12,000–24,000 UZS) — unlocking the occasional paid post or bot feature.</li>
      <li><strong>500–1,000 Stars</strong> (120,000–240,000 UZS) — regular use, gifts, supporting creators.</li>
      <li><strong>2,500+</strong> — channel owners running giveaways or funding ads.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Since Stars never expire, buying slightly more than you need costs you nothing but the timing. Full price table on the <a href="/en/stars">Telegram Stars page</a>.</p>
  </section>

